                <pb facs="0211" n="207" sameAs="frus:frus1938v04/pg207"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.115 Standard Vacuum Oil Company/80:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1938-12-16T14:00:00+08:00">December 16, 1938—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 16—2 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1478. On December 12, I requested the Acting Japanese Consul General to arrange
                    with the Japanese naval authorities for the Standard Vacuum Oil Company’s motor
                    vessel <hi rendition="#italic">Meiyun</hi> to proceed from Kiukiang to Shanghai
                    for much needed overhaul. I suggested that the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Meiyun</hi> proceed down river with the U. S. S. <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Guam</hi> which is scheduled to leave Hankow for Shanghai on or about
                    December 19.</p>
                <p>I have now been orally informed by the Japanese Consulate General that the
                    Japanese naval authorities state they are unable to grant permission because
                    similar requests made on behalf of British vessels have already been refused. I
                    am awaiting receipt of a written explanation but desire to report this case as a
                    particular instance of Japanese refusal to permit navigation on the Yangtze by
                    an American commercial vessel even for the purpose of repairs and as a further
                    instance of Japanese interference with American rights.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Tokyo and Hankow. To Peiping by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
